  "before_interfaces": {
    "lem-integrable-signed-function-integration-against-density": [
      "## Statement\n\nLet $\\mu$ be a positive measure, let $p:X\\to[0,\\infty)$ be finite-valued\nand measurable, and suppose $P(A)=\\int_Ap\\,d\\mu$ is a probability measure.\nIf $f:X\\to\\mathbb R$ is measurable and $|f|\\le M$ for some finite\n$M\\ge0$, then $f$ is integrable under $P$, $fp$ is integrable under $\\mu$,\nand\n$$\\int_X f\\,dP=\\int_X fp\\,d\\mu.$$"
    ],
    "lem-likelihood-ratio-threshold-exists": [
      "## Statement\n\nLet $Q$ be a probability measure on $(X,\\mathcal A)$, and let\n$T:X\\to[0,\\infty)$ be a finite-valued measurable function. For every\n$\\alpha\\in(0,1]$, there exist $c\\in[0,\\infty)$ and\n$\\gamma\\in[0,1]$ such that\n$$Q(T>c)+\\gamma Q(T=c)=\\alpha.$$"
    ],
    "lem-common-support-likelihood-ratio-is-measurable": [
      "## Statement\n\nLet $p_0,p_1:X\\to[0,\\infty)$ be finite-valued measurable functions on a\nmeasurable space $(X,\\mathcal A)$. Define\n$$R(x)=\\begin{cases}p_1(x)/p_0(x),&p_0(x)>0,\\\\0,&p_0(x)=0.\\end{cases}$$\nThen $R$ is finite-valued, nonnegative, and measurable."
    ],
    "def-p-value": [
      "## Definition\n\nLet $\\mathsf E=((\\mathcal X,\\mathcal A),\\Theta,(P_\\theta)_{\\theta\\in\\Theta})$\nbe a statistical experiment ([[def-statistical-experiment]]). A **p-value\nstatistic** is a statistic $p:\\mathcal X\\to[0,1]$, where $[0,1]$ carries the\ntrace of the Borel sigma-algebra, meaning\n$\\{B\\cap[0,1]:B\\in\\mathcal B(\\mathbb R)\\}$\n([[def-statistic-and-estimator]],\n[[def-measurable-function-between-measurable-spaces]]).\n\nFor a nonempty null hypothesis $\\Theta_0\\subseteq\\Theta$, the statistic is a\n**valid p-value for $\\Theta_0$** if it is superuniform under every null law:\n$$P_\\theta(\\{x:p(x)\\le u\\})\\le u\\qquad\\text{for every }\\theta\\in\\Theta_0\\text{ and }u\\in[0,1].$$\nThe variable $p(X)$ is the observed p-value when the data are $X$. The\nvalidity condition is distributional; measurability alone does not imply it."
    ],
    "lem-measurable-rejection-function-defines-randomized-test": [
      "## Statement\n\nLet $\\mathsf E=((X,\\mathcal A),\\Theta,(P_\\theta)_{\\theta\\in\\Theta})$ be a\nstatistical experiment ([[def-statistical-experiment]]) and let\n$\\varphi:X\\to[0,1]$ be measurable, where $[0,1]$ has its Borel sigma-algebra.\nOn the binary action space $\\{0,1\\}$ with its full sigma-algebra, define\n$$K(x,B)=\\varphi(x)\\mathbf 1_B(1)+(1-\\varphi(x))\\mathbf 1_B(0)\\qquad (B\\subseteq\\{0,1\\}).$$\nThen $K$ is a probability kernel and hence a randomized procedure; its\nrandomized-test function under the convention $1=\\mathrm{reject}$ is\n$\\varphi$."
    ],
    "lem-test-zero-one-loss-risk-identities": [
      "## Statement\n\nLet $\\Theta_0,\\Theta_1$ be disjoint subsets of a parameter set $\\Theta$ in\na statistical experiment. Let $K$ be a randomized test on the binary action\nspace $\\{0,1\\}$, with $1$ meaning reject, and write\n$\\varphi(x)=K(x,\\{1\\})$. Define the zero-one error loss by\n$$L(\\theta,a)=\\mathbf 1_{\\Theta_0}(\\theta)a+\\mathbf 1_{\\Theta_1}(\\theta)(1-a),$$\nso $L$ is zero when $\\theta\\notin\\Theta_0\\cup\\Theta_1$. For\n$\\beta_\\varphi(\\theta)=E_\\theta[\\varphi(X)]$ and the risk\n$R(\\theta,K)$ of [[def-loss-function-and-risk]],\n$$R(\\theta,K)=\\beta_\\varphi(\\theta)\\quad(\\theta\\in\\Theta_0),\\qquad R(\\theta,K)=1-\\beta_\\varphi(\\theta)\\quad(\\theta\\in\\Theta_1).$$"
    ],
    "def-test-size-and-power": [
      "## Definition\n\nLet $\\mathsf E=((\\mathcal X,\\mathcal A),\\Theta,(P_\\theta)_{\\theta\\in\\Theta})$\nbe a statistical experiment ([[def-statistical-experiment]]). Let\n$\\Theta_0,\\Theta_1$ be nonempty disjoint subsets of $\\Theta$, called the null\nand alternative hypotheses. A hypothesis is **simple** when it is a singleton\nand **composite** when it has at least two elements.\n\nFor a randomized test with test function\n$\\varphi:\\mathcal X\\to[0,1]$ ([[def-randomized-test]]), its **power function**\nis\n$$\\beta(\\theta):=E_\\theta[\\varphi(X)]\\qquad(\\theta\\in\\Theta).$$\nIts **size** is\n$$\\sup_{\\theta\\in\\Theta_0}\\beta(\\theta),$$\nand it has **level $\\alpha$** when this supremum is at most\n$\\alpha\\in[0,1]$. The expectations exist and lie in $[0,1]$ because\n$0\\le\\varphi\\le1$ and each $P_\\theta$ is a probability measure\n([[def-probability-measure]],\n[[def-expectation-of-a-nonnegative-or-integrable-random-variable]]).\nThe supremum exists because $\\Theta_0$ is nonempty, the values lie in\n$[0,1]$, and $\\mathbb R$ has the least-upper-bound property\n([[def-complete-ordered-field]]).\n\nFor the binary action $a\\in\\{0,1\\}$, where $1$ means reject, the\n**zero-one error loss** is $1$ when $a=1$ and $\\theta\\in\\Theta_0$, or when\n$a=0$ and $\\theta\\in\\Theta_1$, and is $0$ otherwise. The decision-rule risk\nfrom [[def-loss-function-and-risk]] integrates this loss against the action\nkernel and data law. For each fixed $\\theta$, its section on the binary\naction space is measurable. The risk identities proved in\n[[lem-test-zero-one-loss-risk-identities]] show that risk equals\n$\\beta(\\theta)$ on $\\Theta_0$ and $1-\\beta(\\theta)$ on $\\Theta_1$. Thus size\nis the worst null error risk, and power is the rejection probability under\neach alternative parameter."
    ],
    "def-likelihood-ratio-test-with-boundary-randomization": [
      "## Definition\n\nLet $\\mathsf E=((\\mathcal X,\\mathcal A),\\{0,1\\},(P_0,P_1))$ be the\ntwo-law statistical experiment ([[def-statistical-experiment]]). Assume\n$P_0,P_1$ are both absolutely continuous with respect to a supplied\nsigma-finite measure $\\mu$ on $(\\mathcal X,\\mathcal A)$\n([[def-dominated-statistical-experiment]]) and specify finite-valued,\nnonnegative measurable RN density representatives $p_0=dP_0/d\\mu$ and\n$p_1=dP_1/d\\mu$ ([[def-likelihood-density]]). Under the Axiom of Choice\n([[def-axiom-of-choice]]),\n[[def-likelihood-density]] supplies such representatives for arbitrary\ndominated laws. If the representatives are given as part of the setup, the\nconstruction below requires no choice.\n\nDefine the common-support likelihood ratio\n$$R(x)=\\begin{cases}p_1(x)/p_0(x),&p_0(x)>0,\\\\0,&p_0(x)=0.\\end{cases}$$\nThe result [[lem-common-support-likelihood-ratio-is-measurable]] shows that\n$R$ is finite-valued, nonnegative, and measurable. The set\n$\\{p_0=0<p_1\\}$ is kept separate from the ratio region.\n\nFor $\\alpha=0$, define the rejection function\n$$\\varphi^*_0(x)=\\mathbf 1_{\\{p_0=0<p_1\\}}(x).$$\nFor $0<\\alpha\\le1$, choose $c\\ge0$ and $\\gamma\\in[0,1]$ satisfying\n$$P_0(R>c)+\\gamma P_0(R=c)=\\alpha,$$\nwhose existence follows from\n[[lem-likelihood-ratio-threshold-exists]] applied to $Q=P_0$ and $T=R$.\nDefine\n$$\n\\varphi^*_\\alpha(x)=\\begin{cases}1,&p_0(x)=0<p_1(x),\\\\1,&p_0(x)>0\\text{ and }R(x)>c,\\\\\\gamma,&p_0(x)>0\\text{ and }R(x)=c,\\\\0,&\\text{otherwise}.\\end{cases}\n$$\nThis specifies the candidate likelihood-ratio rejection function."
    ],
    "thm-p-value-superuniformity": [
      "## Statement\n\nLet $p$ be a valid p-value for a nonempty null hypothesis $\\Theta_0$ in a\nstatistical experiment. For every $\\alpha\\in[0,1]$, the deterministic test\nwith rejection function\n$$\\varphi_\\alpha(x)=\\mathbf 1_{\\{p(x)\\le\\alpha\\}}$$\nhas size at most $\\alpha$, and therefore has level $\\alpha$."
    ],
    "lem-likelihood-ratio-test-is-measurable-and-exact-size": [
      "## Statement\n\nIn the setup of [[def-likelihood-ratio-test-with-boundary-randomization]],\nfor every $\\alpha\\in[0,1]$ the candidate rejection function\n$\\varphi^*_\\alpha$ is measurable into $[0,1]$, defines a randomized test,\nand has size exactly $\\alpha$ for the simple null $P_0$."
    ],
    "thm-neyman-pearson-lemma": [
      "## Statement\n\nLet $\\mathsf E=((\\mathcal X,\\mathcal A),\\{0,1\\},(P_0,P_1))$ be the\ntwo-law experiment with simple null $P_0$ and simple alternative $P_1$.\nAssume both laws are dominated by a common sigma-finite measure $\\mu$ and\nassume the Axiom of Choice, so finite nonnegative density representatives\n$p_0,p_1$ are supplied by [[def-likelihood-density]]. For every\n$\\alpha\\in[0,1]$, let $\\varphi_\\alpha^*$ be the likelihood-ratio test with\nboundary randomization from\n[[def-likelihood-ratio-test-with-boundary-randomization]]. Then every\nrandomized test $\\varphi$ satisfying\n$E_{P_0}[\\varphi(X)]\\le\\alpha$ also satisfies\n$$E_{P_1}[\\varphi(X)]\\le E_{P_1}[\\varphi_\\alpha^*(X)].$$\nNo absolute-continuity assumption $P_1\\ll P_0$ is imposed; the test rejects\non $\\{p_0=0<p_1\\}$."
    ],
